```java
class Singleton {
    private static Singleton instance;   // NOT volatile — the bug

    static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {                 // first check, no lock
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {          // second check, locked
                    instance = new Singleton();  // can be reordered!
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

`new Singleton()` is really three steps — allocate memory, run the constructor, assign the reference — and without `volatile`, the compiler or CPU is free to reorder the reference assignment ahead of the constructor finishing. A second thread's unlocked first check can then see a non-null `instance` pointing at a half-constructed object, and read fields that haven't been set yet.

```java
class Singleton {
    private static volatile Singleton instance;

    static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

The only change is `volatile` on the field. Since Java 5's memory model revision, a write to a volatile field cannot be reordered ahead of any write that happens before it in program order, and a read of that field is guaranteed to see all writes that happened before it — so a thread that sees a non-null `instance` is guaranteed to see a fully constructed object.

```java
class Singleton {
    private static class Holder {
        static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();
    }
    static Singleton getInstance() {
        return Holder.INSTANCE;
    }
}
```

This sidesteps the whole reordering question: the JVM guarantees a class is only initialized once, and that initialization happens-before any thread first accesses it — so `Holder.INSTANCE` is safely published with no explicit locking or `volatile` at all. Simpler and equally correct; see [thread-safe singleton implementation](iv-thread-safe-singleton-implementation) for the full comparison of approaches, including when you'd still choose DCL over this.
